00:00:04.999 --> 00:00:07.820 align:start position:0%
50 meters underwater just off the Greek  island of Antikythera lie the remains of  a merchant vessel that sank 2,000 years  ago it's one of the oldest and most  famous shipwrecks ever discovered when  divers returned to the wreck this summer  they found something incredibly rare  they had found a human skeleton that's  been buried under pottery shards and  sand for two millennia the bodies of  shipwreck victims are usually swept away  by ocean currents or eaten by fish so  this is an exciting find for  archaeologists they hope to learn more  about the people who traveled alongside  the ships rich cargo previously divers  have found bronze Spears  wine jars and most famously a  sophisticated clockwork device dubbed  the Antikythera mechanism using a tube  to suck up sediment the divers carefully  excavated there find a partial skull  with three teeth arm bones some rib  pieces and two femur the fragile bones  were transferred to ziplock bags full of  seawater before being brought to the  surface bone expert Hanna's Schroeder  was the first to examine them you know  there's those line creasing lines on top  like salt so kind of flaky probably from  the sediment there's iron concretions  off in office right which we're  suspecting it could be part of his gun  his or her garment the skeleton was  found next to pieces of corroded iron  perhaps clasps from the individuals  clothing or shackles for a slave you can  see red patches where the iron oxide has  stained the bones the skull also hints  at the individuals age probably mid 20s  because the bones are not fully knitted  together the teeth show little wear and  the two femurs or leg bones look fairly  robust so Hanna's guesses this was a  young man  but was he crew a rich passenger or even  a slave to learn more about him Hanna's  hopes to extract DNA from the skull a  skull that was underwater for 2,000  years this will push the boundaries of  ancient DNA analysis if it works we  could learn about his hair and eye color  diseases he might have inherited and  perhaps where he came from this could  help answer questions about the ship  itself it was sailing towards Rome when  it sank but archaeologists think it was  built in Greece and had a Greek crew if  the DNA analysis indicates that the  young man had Greek ancestry it would  add weight to this theory and another  piece in the story of this ancient  tragedy  you
 

